{
  "solution": "<p>Let the total sum of the donation be represented by N. Based on the problem description, N is an integer in US dollars and must fulfill the following criteria:</p><p>1.  The donation amount N must be strictly greater than 100 and strictly less than 500. This can be written as 100 < N < 500.</p><p>2.  When N is divided by 7, the remainder is 3. This relationship is expressed using modular arithmetic as N ≡ 3 (mod 7).</p><p>3.  When N is divided by 11, the remainder is 5. This relationship is expressed as N ≡ 5 (mod 11).</p><p>We are looking for the smallest integer N that satisfies all three of these conditions.</p><p>From the second condition, N can be written in the form N = 7k + 3 for some integer k.<br>From the third condition, N can be written in the form N = 11m + 5 for some integer m.</p><p>To find a value of N that satisfies both congruences simultaneously, we equate the two expressions for N:<br>7k + 3 = 11m + 5<br>Rearranging the terms to isolate the variables on different sides gives:<br>7k = 11m + 2</p><p>We need to find integer values for k and m that satisfy this equation. We can look for the smallest non-negative integer values of m that make the right side (11m + 2) divisible by 7. Let`s test values for m starting from 0:</p><p>If m = 0, 11(0) + 2 = 2 (Not divisible by 7)<br>If m = 1, 11(1) + 2 = 13 (Not divisible by 7)<br>If m = 2, 11(2) + 2 = 24 (Not divisible by 7)<br>If m = 3, 11(3) + 2 = 33 + 2 = 35 (Divisible by 7. 35 / 7 = 5)</p><p>The smallest non-negative integer value for m that yields an integer k is m = 3. For m = 3, we find k = 5.</p><p>Now substitute m = 3 back into the expression N = 11m + 5:<br>N = 11(3) + 5 = 33 + 5 = 38.</p><p>Alternatively, substitute k = 5 back into the expression N = 7k + 3:<br>N = 7(5) + 3 = 35 + 3 = 38.</p><p>So, N = 38 is the smallest positive integer that satisfies both N ≡ 3 (mod 7) and N ≡ 5 (mod 11). This is a particular solution to the system of congruences.</p><p>The general solution for N satisfying both congruences is given by N ≡ N_particular (mod lcm(modulus1, modulus2)). In this case, it is N ≡ 38 (mod lcm(7, 11)).<br>Since 7 and 11 are prime numbers, their least common multiple (lcm) is their product: lcm(7, 11) = 7 * 11 = 77.</p><p>Therefore, the general form of any integer N that satisfies both remainder conditions is N = 77j + 38, where j is an integer.</p><p>Now, we must find the smallest value of N of the form 77j + 38 that falls within the specified range 100 < N < 500.</p><p>We set up the inequality:<br>100 < 77j + 38 < 500</p><p>To isolate j, subtract 38 from all parts of the inequality:<br>100 - 38 < 77j < 500 - 38<br>62 < 77j < 462</p><p>Now, divide all parts by 77:<br>62 / 77 < j < 462 / 77</p><p>Calculating the division:<br>0.805... < j < 6</p><p>Since j must be an integer, the possible values for j are 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5.</p><p>We are seeking the smallest possible integer sum N within the range, which corresponds to the smallest possible integer value of j that satisfies the inequality. The smallest such integer is j = 1.</p><p>Substitute the smallest valid value of j (j = 1) back into the general form for N:<br>N = 77(1) + 38 = 77 + 38 = 115.</p><p>Let`s verify that N = 115 meets all conditions:<br>- Is 100 < 115 < 500? Yes.<br>- Is the remainder 3 when 115 is divided by 7? 115 = 16 * 7 + 3. Yes.<br>- Is the remainder 5 when 115 is divided by 11? 115 = 10 * 11 + 5. Yes.</p><p>Since 115 is the smallest integer N within the specified range that satisfies both remainder conditions, it is the amount allocated for immediate disbursement.</p>",
  "answer": "115"
}
