  "solution": "Let the three distinct prime numbers in the set S be $p_1, p_2, p_3$. The question asks for the value of $p_1 + p_2 + p_3$.<br/><br/>Statement (1) says that the product of the numbers in S is less than 100. So, $p_1 p_2 p_3 < 100$. We need to check if this condition restricts the possible sets of three distinct primes to a unique sum.<br/>Possible sets of three distinct primes with product less than 100:<br/>- {2, 3, 5}: Product $2 \\times 3 \\times 5 = 30 < 100$. Sum = $2+3+5 = 10$.<br/>- {2, 3, 7}: Product $2 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 42 < 100$. Sum = $2+3+7 = 12$.<br/>- {2, 3, 11}: Product $2 \\times 3 \\times 11 = 66 < 100$. Sum = $2+3+11 = 16$.<br/>- {2, 3, 13}: Product $2 \\times 3 \\times 13 = 78 < 100$. Sum = $2+3+13 = 18$.<br/>- {2, 5, 7}: Product $2 \\times 5 \\times 7 = 70 < 100$. Sum = $2+5+7 = 14$.<br/>Any other set of three distinct primes will have a product of 100 or more (e.g., {2, 3, 17} product is 102; {2, 5, 11} product is 110; {3, 5, 7} product is 105).<br/>Since there are multiple possible sets of primes (and thus multiple possible sums) that satisfy statement (1), statement (1) alone is not sufficient.<br/><br/>Statement (2) says that 3 is one of the numbers in S. This tells us one of the primes is 3, but the other two can be any two distinct primes other than 3. For example, the set could be {3, 2, 5} (sum 10), {3, 2, 7} (sum 12), {3, 5, 7} (sum 15), {3, 2, 101} (sum 106), etc. There are infinitely many possibilities for the other two distinct primes, leading to infinitely many possible sums. Therefore, statement (2) alone is not sufficient.<br/><br/>Now consider both statements together. The set S contains three distinct prime numbers, one of which is 3, and their product is less than 100.<br/>Let the set be {3, $p_2$, $p_3$}, where $p_2$ and $p_3$ are distinct primes and neither is 3. The product is $3 \\times p_2 \\times p_3 < 100$, which means $p_2 \\times p_3 < 100/3 \\approx 33.33$. We need to find distinct primes $p_2, p_3$ (neither equal to 3) such that their product is less than 33.33. Assume $p_2 < p_3$.<br/>If $p_2 = 2$ (prime, not 3): $2 \\times p_3 < 33.33 \implies p_3 < 16.66$. $p_3$ must be a prime distinct from 2 and 3, and $p_3 > p_2=2$. Possible values for $p_3$: 5, 7, 11, 13.<br/>- If $p_3 = 5$, the set is {3, 2, 5}. Product 30, Sum 10. Valid.<br/>- If $p_3 = 7$, the set is {3, 2, 7}. Product 42, Sum 12. Valid.<br/>- If $p_3 = 11$, the set is {3, 2, 11}. Product 66, Sum 16. Valid.<br/>- If $p_3 = 13$, the set is {3, 2, 13}. Product 78, Sum 18. Valid.<br/>If $p_2 = 5$ (prime, not 3, $p_2 > 2$): $5 \\times p_3 < 33.33 \implies p_3 < 6.66$. $p_3$ must be a prime distinct from 3 and 5, and $p_3 > p_2=5$. No such primes exist (primes less than 6.66 are 2, 3, 5).<br/>If $p_2 = 7$ (prime, not 3, $p_2 > 5$): $7 \\times p_3 < 33.33 \implies p_3 < 4.76$. $p_3$ must be a prime distinct from 3 and 7, and $p_3 > p_2=7$. No such primes exist.<br/>The possible sets satisfying both statements are {2, 3, 5}, {2, 3, 7}, {2, 3, 11}, and {2, 3, 13}. These sets result in sums of 10, 12, 16, and 18, respectively. Since there is more than one possible value for the sum, even with both statements combined, the statements together are not sufficient to determine a unique sum.<br/><br/>Therefore, statements (1) and (2) together are not sufficient.",
  "answer": "E"
}
